  "preamble": "Implosivization of a voiced labial stop: [b] is produced with a lowering, closed glottis and becomes the implosive [ɓ]. In Sindhi and Saraiki, Middle Indo-Aryan initial voiced stops and voiced geminates (-bb-) became implosives, a highly distinctive change within New Indo-Aryan (Masica 1991). In Nguni Bantu (Zulu, Xhosa), Proto-Bantu *b is continued as the implosive written b (Zulu ubaba /úɓaːɓá/), while plain [b] occurs after a nasal (mb) and as bh; since the phonetic value of Proto-Bantu *b is uncertain (it may have been [β] in some environments), the exact starting point of the Nguni change is open. In Vietnamese, Proto-Vietic *b also ends up as ɓ, but only indirectly, via devoicing to p and then p > ɓ (see p > ɓ).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Implosivization, Glottalization",
